<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kahuna">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kahuna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kahuna nui, kahuna lapaʻau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kahuna is a Hawaiian word for an expert in any field, used historically for doctors, surgeons and dentists as well as priests, ministers and sorcerers, and for masters of crafts such as canoe building, navigation and carving; it is glossed as 'keeper of hidden knowledge', from kahu, 'caretaker', and huna, 'secret'. According to Fornander the priesthood had ten branches, and a master of all ten could become kahuna nui, high priest, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a> was to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>. Some kahuna practices were outlawed from 1831 to 1863 and from 1887 to 1919; since then all have been legal except sorcery, which was decriminalised in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kahuna.jpg" alt="Kahuna" width="330" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hawaiian_Kahuna_Kahiko_(PP-33-11-023).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A kahuna might be versed in agriculture, canoe building or any other skill or field of knowledge, and the secrecy surrounding their knowledge has been compared to that of the Freemasons and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> masters of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Communities called on them to bless new buildings and construction projects or to officiate at weddings. The book Tales from the Night Rainbow lists forty types of kahuna, twenty of them in healing alone, among them the kahuna hāhā, an expert who diagnosed sickness or pain by feeling the body.</p><p>Classes of kahuna in pre-contact <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> included the kahuna kilokilo, a reader of skies and omens; the kahuna kalai wa‘a, a canoe maker; the kahuna ho‘okele, a navigator; the kahuna kumu hula, leader of a hula group; the kahuna ka‘a kaua, a war strategist; and the kahuna lawelawe iwi, who cared for the bones of the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Of Fornander's ten branches of the priesthood, the ʻAnāʻanā, Hoʻopiopio and Hoʻounāunā were said to bring death or injury to others by prayer, and the Hoʻokomokomo and Poʻi ʻUhane to use spirits for divination and possession, while the Kuhikuhi puʻuone located sites for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">heiau</a>, or temples. Kahuna nui usually lived in places such as Waimea Valley, known as the Valley of the Priests, and were given slices of land running from the mountain to the sea.</p><p>Craft kahuna were never prohibited, but as native Hawaiian culture declined many died without passing on their knowledge; when the Hōkūleʻa was built to sail to the South Pacific, the master navigator Mau Piailug of Satawal was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> to reteach navigation. King Kamehameha IV, in his translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>, used kahuna for Anglican priests and kahunapule for lay and ordained ministers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Outsiders distorted the word into a witch or wizard. In the 1959 film Gidget the Big Kahuna was the leader of a group of surfers, and the figure became commonplace in the beach party films of the 1960s; the Hawaiian surfer Duke Kahanamoku rejected the title because he knew its original meaning. The New Age system called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna</a> uses kahuna for a person of priestly or shamanic standing, which has shaped English dictionary definitions. The Native Hawaiian scholar Lisa Kahaleole Hall wrote that Huna bears absolutely no resemblance to any Hawaiian worldview or spiritual practice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kahuna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1867 the <b>kahuna</b> Kūpaʻiulu of Maui described a counter-sorcery ritual to heal someone made ill by hoʻopiʻopiʻo, another person's evil thoughts: a kapa cloth was shaken and prayers were said, and if the evil spirit appeared and possessed the patient, the practitioner could save them by speaking…</blockquote><a href="/religion/hawaiian-religion">Hawaiian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Priests and experts (<b>kahuna</b>) served the chiefs, the commoners (makaʻāinana) farmed and fished for them all, and the outcast kauwā stood below everyone.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>Priests (<b>kahuna</b>) of the Hawaiian religion recited the Kumulipo during the makahiki season, which honoured the god Lono.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a></li></ul></section>
